# 🧪 constructs 🐠 directly (a same-class call): 🐠's own initializer owns the receiver, so it must own and free its
# dynamically allocated generic description on failure, not this call site.
#
# A call site does emit a guarded pending-description cleanup (#118) at each later checkpoint, but the call disarms
# it (stores null into its tracking variable) once the initializer is entered, so after promotion to registers that
# cleanup reads `ejcFreeDescription(ptr null)`. None of 🧪's, 🧬's or 🦈's arguments can reraise before the
# initializer is entered, so any free of an actual description value (`ptr %…`) there would be a post-entry double
# free. 🧯 below covers the pre-entry case, where the call site must free it.
@ 1f9ea-
+ call ptr @ejcAllocDescription\(
- call void @ejcFreeDescription\(ptr %

# 🐠's own initializer frees the dynamic description it stored on the receiver before releasing the receiver, but
# only on the path where the receiver's runtime class info matches its own (never for a static description).
@ _\.class_1f420\.init
+ call void @ejcFreeDescription\(

# 🔬 constructs 🐟, which directly inherits 🐠's initializer without declaring one of its own: the receiver's
# runtime class info then never matches 🐠's, so 🐠's initializer never frees the description it was given. This
# call site is the only remaining owner and must free it itself on failure (#133).
@ 1f52c-
+ call ptr @ejcAllocDescription\(
+ call void @ejcFreeDescription\(

# 🧫 does the same for 🦐, one level further from the class that declares the initializer.
@ 1f9eb-
+ call ptr @ejcAllocDescription\(
+ call void @ejcFreeDescription\(

# 🧬 constructs 🦑, which owns a frame of its own (it declares a field and calls 🐠's initializer explicitly via
# super), so this call site must not free the description itself: 🦑's own initializer frees it exactly once,
# whether it fails during the super call or after it.
@ 1f9ec-
+ call ptr @ejcAllocDescription\(
- call void @ejcFreeDescription\(ptr %

# 🧯 constructs 🦑 like 🧬, but with an argument that can reraise before 🦑's initializer is entered. Exactly one free
# of the live description must remain: the one on that pre-entry reraise path, where this call site still owns it.
# The cleanups after the initializer call must be disarmed (`ptr null`); a second live free would free it again
# after 🦑's own frame took ownership. The receiver is likewise released without deinitialization only on that path.
@ 1f9ef-
+ call ptr @ejcAllocDescription\(
= 1 call void @ejcFreeDescription\(ptr %
= 1 call void @ejcReleaseWithoutDeinit\(ptr %alloc\)

# 🦑's initializer has two independent failure paths that each free the description exactly once: the super call
# to 🐠 failing, and 🦑's own raise after a successful super call. Requiring exactly two matches (rather than just
# "at least one") means deleting either path's free is caught, unlike a plain presence check.
@ _\.class_1f991\.init
= 2 call void @ejcFreeDescription\(

# 🦈 constructs 🐳, which owns a frame of its own like 🦑, so this call site must not free the description either.
@ 1f988-
+ call ptr @ejcAllocDescription\(
- call void @ejcFreeDescription\(ptr %

# 🐳 forms a multi-level explicit-super chain (🐳 ➡️ 🦑 ➡️ 🐠) and can fail before, during, or after its own call to
# 🦑's initializer. Each of the three paths frees the shared description exactly once, from 🐳's own frame (the
# intermediate 🦑/🐠 frames never own it here, since the receiver's runtime class info is 🐳's, not theirs).
# Requiring exactly three matches catches removing the free on any one of the three paths.
@ _\.class_1f433\.init
= 3 call void @ejcFreeDescription\(
